<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clotilda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Clotilde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Clotilda was a two-masted schooner and the last known American slave ship to bring enslaved Africans to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, landing 110 men, women and children at Mobile Bay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, in autumn 1859 or on 9 July 1860, long after Congress had banned the trade in 1807. Built for the Mobile shipyard owner Timothy Meaher and commanded by William Foster, it bought its captives at Whydah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, whose forces had raided communities in the interior. The ship was then burned and scuttled to destroy the evidence. After the Civil War, Oluale Kossola (Cudjo Lewis) and 31 other survivors founded Africatown north of Mobile; the wreck was identified in 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clotilda-slave-ship.jpg" alt="Clotilda" width="330" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wreck_of_the_Slave_Ship_Clotilda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Timothy Meaher had the Clotilda built in 1855 or 1856 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-sheathed schooner 86 feet long, designed for the lumber trade. He had learned that West African peoples were at war and that the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> was willing to sell prisoners, brought from raids in the interior to the slave market at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a>. Meaher was said to have wagered a wealthy man from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> that he could smuggle Africans into the country despite the Act Prohibiting Importation of Slaves of 1807. The schooner was refitted with a false deck, and William Foster carried papers falsely stating that he was delivering lumber.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Foster sailed from Mobile on 4 March 1860 with a crew of 12, including himself, and reached Whydah on 15 May. A hurricane off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermuda">Bermuda</a> had damaged the ship on the way, and the crew, who had found the hidden deck, were promised double pay, which Foster never gave them. He offered 100 dollars each for 125 captives, chosen from about four thousand held in a warehouse, but seeing two steamers off the port he left with 110 on board. Research in the 21st century suggests that many were Takpa or Tapa, the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> name for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> people of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><p>On the voyage back the schooner escaped a man o' war in a squall and was disguised as a coastal trader. Foster brought it into the Port of Mobile at night, transferred the captives to a river steamboat and burned the Clotilda before sinking it. Meaher kept 30 of the captives, and the rest went to the venture's financial backers or were sold further away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1861 the federal government prosecuted Meaher and Foster in Mobile for illegal importation, but the case was dismissed for lack of evidence, perhaps also because of the outbreak of the Civil War. After emancipation the community of Africatown kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and its own customs into the 1950s; Kossola lived until 1935, Redoshi (Sally Smith) until 1937 and Matilda McCrear until 1940. The reporter Ben Raines and a team from the University of Southern Mississippi surveyed the 12 Mile Island section of the Mobile River in 2018, and the Alabama Historical Commission announced the find on 22 May 2019. Zora Neale Hurston's Barracoon, an account of Kossola's life completed in 1931, was published in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clotilda (slave ship)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The trade outlasted Britain’s treaty of 1852: in 1860 the <b>Clotilda</b> carried 110 captives from Whydah to Alabama, among them Oluale Kossola.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dahomey">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Dahomey</a></li></ul></section>
